Tim needs to make 4 gallons of lemonade, and he wants to make an additional gallon that is twice as tart as the rest. So, the total amount of lemonade he'll need to make is 4 + 1 = 5 gallons.

For a gallon of lemonade, the recipe calls for 1 cup of fresh lemon juice.  Therefore, for 5 gallons of lemonade he will need 5 cups of lemon juice.

He knows that 6 lemons yield 1 cup of juice. So to find how many lemons he needs for 5 cups of lemon juice, we can set up a proportion:

$\frac{6 \text{ lemons}}{1 \text{ cup}} = \frac{x \text{ lemons}}{5 \text{ cups}}$

Cross-multiplying, we get:

$6 \cdot 5 = 1 \cdot x$
$30 = x$

So, Tim needs 30 lemons to make 5 gallons of lemonade, including the extra gallon of lemonade.

Alternatively, if Tim makes 4 gallons of lemonade at regular tartness and 1 gallon of lemonade at twice tartness, then he needs to make an extra gallon of lemonade that is twice as tart. We know that for one gallon of lemonade: 1 cup lemon juice, 6 lemons. We can calculate the lemon juice needed for the additional gallon: it will be twice as tart therefore twice : 2 cups of lemon juice.
So, the number of lemons required for these 2 cups is
$\frac{6 \text{ lemon}}{1 \text{ cup}} = \frac{x \text{ lemon}}{2 \text{ cups}}$
$ x = 2 \cdot \frac{6}{1} = 12.$
He needs 12 lemons for the extra gallon.
